{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787990732,"nonce":"/SQbY7ZfNoOeaI1rWssX9K7t6pk=","title":"Every `*UpsertedData` frame payload in the contract disagrees with its only producer, and no gate can see it because a frame payload is a `map[string]any` with no Go struct to reflect","message":"Three frame kinds are published on the SSE spine by `internal/alerts/service`, and all three payloads fail the schema the contract declares for them. `case.upserted` is the worst of the three because it disagrees on a value and not merely on a key: `CaseUpsertedData.state` is `$ref: State` — `firing | suppressed | resolved | expired` — while `caseFramePayload` emits `o.State().String()`, which since ADR 0040 is `open | closed`. `open` is not a member of the enum the contract says that field holds, and the correct enum is two thousand lines away in the same document: `CaseState`, whose own description warns *\"It is not a narrower `State`. `firing` and `suppressed` are not values missing from this enum; they are facts about the ALERT.\"* The contract makes exactly the mistake its own prose forbids.\n\nThe key-level drift is the same story in three places. `CaseUpsertedData` requires `[alert_id, seq, state, ack_state, started_at]` and the producer emits three keys: `alert_id`, `state`, `ack`. Three of the five required are absent, and the acknowledgement is spelled `ack` where the contract declares `ack_state` — so the field is not renamed, it is missing and a different one is present. `AlertUpsertedData` requires `[state, alertname, last_seen_at]` and no producer emits `alertname` or `last_seen_at` at all. `EventAppendedData` requires `[type, occurred_at, recorded_at, actor_kind, summary]` and `eventFramePayload` emits one of them.\n\n**The demonstration.** Run, not read. The three payload maps were transcribed field for field from their builders into a throwaway vitest file and parsed with the CHECKED-IN generated validators — `CaseUpsertedDataSchema`, `AlertUpsertedDataSchema`, `EventAppendedDataSchema` from `web/src/api/generated/validators.ts`, the artefact gate G4 regenerates and diffs. Every one failed:\n\n    case.upserted  -\u003e seq: Expected \"seq\" but received undefined\n                      state: Expected (\"firing\" | \"suppressed\" | \"resolved\" | \"expired\") but received \"open\"\n                      ack_state: Expected \"ack_state\" but received undefined\n                      started_at: Expected \"started_at\" but received undefined\n    alert.upserted -\u003e alertname: Expected \"alertname\" but received undefined\n                      last_seen_at: Expected \"last_seen_at\" but received undefined\n    event.appended -\u003e occurred_at, recorded_at, actor_kind, summary: all received undefined\n\nTwo things that reading alone would have got wrong, and which bound the claim. The undeclared keys — `ack`, `alert_key`, `snoozed_until`, `group_id`, `case_id` — raise no issue at all, because every generated payload schema is `v.looseObject`; the extra keys are a documentation gap and not a validation failure. And `alert.upserted` reports only two issues rather than three, because `state: \"firing\"` genuinely is a legal `State` — the alert frame's one shared field is the one field in this whole finding that is correct.\n\n**Why it matters.** Nothing is broken on screen today, and the ticket should not be read as claiming otherwise. `live.tsx:67-73` uses `case.upserted` purely as a cache-invalidation trigger and never touches `frame.data`, and `stream.ts:369` admits a frame on `isFrame`, an envelope shape check over `kind` and `seq` that never reaches the payload. The published schema is therefore describing a payload no code on either side reads.\n\nThat is precisely what makes it expensive later. The contract is the artefact this repository generates two clients from and holds to four gates; it is what a second consumer — another UI, a CLI, a customer integration — would build against, and it promises five fields where three arrive and declares an enum that cannot contain the value sent. A consumer that validated frames the way `client.ts` validates responses would reject every `case.upserted` oto has ever emitted. The generated `CaseUpsertedDataSchema` already exists, checked in and unused; the day somebody wires it into the stream path believing it describes reality, the live view stops updating.\n\nThe gate gap is the real finding. G1 reflects every exported `*DTO`/`*Request`/`*Query` struct and diffs it against the contract, which is why the response bodies cannot drift — but a frame payload is built as an inline `map[string]any` literal at each call site, so there is no struct to reflect and G1 has nothing to bind. `dto_schema_test.go:1444-1450` already records `UiEventKind` and `UiEventResource` as *\"genuinely uncovered\"* for a related reason; the `*Data` schemas are uncovered too and are not written down anywhere as such. This is a hole of the same shape as the one `TestEveryAPIStructIsBound` exists to close for DTOs: without it a new frame kind is simply not covered and nothing says so.\n\n**Where.** Producers: `internal/alerts/service/service.go:310-316` (`caseFramePayload`, the single builder, reached from `:300` and `lifecycle.go:515`), `:273-285` (`eventFramePayload`, reached from `:255` and `:268`), and the seven hand-built `alert.upserted` literals — `lifecycle.go:519-522` (`{alert_key, state}`), `actions.go:180-182` (`{ack}`), `:475-477` (`{snoozed_until}`), `:580` (`{snoozed_until: nil}`), `sweep.go:314-316` (`{state}`), `:575-577` (`{state}`), `:693-695` (`{snoozed_until}`). Contract: `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:10312-10314` (`AlertUpsertedData`), `:10340-10364` (`CaseUpsertedData`, `state` at `:10355`), `:10391-10393` (`EventAppendedData`); the enums it should have used are `CaseState` at `:5839` and `State` at `:5824`. Consumers: `web/src/api/live.tsx:67-73`, `web/src/api/stream.ts:360-387`. The gate that cannot see it: `test/contract/dto_schema_test.go` (`bindings`, `TestEveryAPIStructIsBound`), with the existing uncovered-pair note at `:1444-1450`. `GroupUpsertedData` at `:10366` is deliberately excluded from this ticket: `alert_groups` was deleted by git-bug `7570090` and `live.tsx:74-82` already documents `group.upserted` as a retired kind that invalidates nothing.\n\n**Scope.** medium — the contract edits are small and mechanical, but choosing between the two directions below is a design decision about what a §E.4 change notice is for, and closing the gate gap properly means giving frame payloads a reflectable Go type.\n\n**Done when.** A direction is chosen and the contract and the producers agree under a gate that would fail if they stopped agreeing. Either the payloads grow to match the contract — in which case `caseFramePayload` gains `seq`, `started_at` and a correctly named `ack_state`, and `CaseUpsertedData.state` is repointed from `State` to `CaseState`, which is a fix required in both directions and on its own is the one-line half of this ticket — or the contract shrinks to match the payloads, which is the reading §E.4 invites, since a frame is documented as a CHANGE NOTICE and not a resource and the client is expected to re-read for detail. If it shrinks, `required` narrows to what is actually sent and the undeclared keys are declared, so a consumer can tell a notice from a payload. Either way each frame payload is built by a named Go type rather than an inline `map[string]any`, that type is added to G1's `bindings` so the drift becomes a build failure, and whatever remains genuinely uncoverable is written into the note at `dto_schema_test.go:1444-1450` beside the two pairs already recorded there.","files":null}]}